  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...Frontal boundary remains stalled across central \nFlorida. Mostly cloudy skies has overall limited daytime heating and \ninstability. However, some breaks in the clouds, a very moist \nairmass (PW values 2.2-2.5\"), and the weak boundary in place will \ncontinue high rain chances (~80-90%) across the area through late \nafternoon/early evening. While isolated strong storms will be \npossible, main concern will be locally heavy to excessive rainfall \nand mostly minor flooding from either repeated rounds of rain or \nslow moving showers/storms. Quick rainfall accumulations of 2-4\" can \nbe expected with this activity, with localized higher amounts of 5+ \ninches possible, which may lead to isolated instances of flash \nflooding. This will especially be true should any training bands of \nheavier rain develop along the coast, mainly north of the Treasure \nCoast, late day into tonight that some of the hi-res guidance has \nbeen hinting at recently. A Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall continues across the area, highlighting this \nthreat.  \n  \nThursday-Friday...Front remains stalled across the area tomorrow, \nlifting slowly northward on Friday as Isaias is forecast to lift \nN/NE over the central Gulf, eventually making landfall as a \nhurricane along the northern Gulf coast Friday night. A very moist \nairmass remains in place across the area, with PW values of 2.2-2.5\" \nremaining through Thursday, decreasing slightly to around 2.0-2.1\" \non Friday. This will keep high rain chances in place across the area \n(up to 80-90% Thursday and 60-80% Friday), with multiple rounds of \nshowers and storms forecast. Locally heavy rainfall and flooding \nissues will continue to be one of the main threats, with a Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall remaining in place \nthrough at least Thursday. Quick rainfall accumulations of 2-4\" will \ncontinue to be possible each day, with convective coverage \nincreasing into the afternoon and early evening with daytime \nheating. Isolated strong storms will still be possible as well, with \nmain threats being lightning strikes and strong gusts to 40 mph. \n\nIncreased cloud cover and high rain chances will keep highs near to \nbelow normal through late week, with highs in the 80s. Overnight \nlows will remain warm and muggy in the 70s. Onshore flow persists, \nwith wind speeds as strong as 10-15 mph along the Volusia County \ncoast Thursday and along much of the coast on Friday. \n\nSaturday-Wednesday...(Modified Previous Discussion) Ridge aloft over \nthe western Atlantic shifts westward and across the area into late \nweekend before strengthening over the northwestern Gulf early next \nweek. Locally, high coverage of showers and storms continues \nSaturday, as the stalled front finally lifts northward on the \neastern periphery of Isaias. Strengthening southerly flow between \nthe ridge and Isaias will continue to advect deep moisture into the \npeninsula, supporting PoPs up to 60-80% through this weekend. Breezy \nand gusty conditions are forecast, especially on Saturday along the \ncoast, making for poor to hazardous beach and boating conditions. As \nthe ridge strengthens over the Gulf, a weak front pushes through the \narea into early next week, with light northerly winds developing, \nfinally reducing PWATs and putting an end to the above normal \ncoverage of showers and storms. In fact, could see mostly dry \nconditions by midweek, with PoPs as low as 10-20% for much of the \narea Tuesday and Wednesday. High temperatures look to increase and \nlinger in the upper 80s/near 90 through the period, though nearer to \nnormal lows are possible next week.",
